{"id": "CA13-001", "question": "Someone applies for a company's shares under a fictitious name. What punishment do they face?", "answer": "They are liable for action for fraud: imprisonment of six months to ten years, plus a fine of not less than the amount involved in the fraud and up to three times that amount. Where the fraud involves public interest the minimum term is higher.", "gold_clauses": ["s38(1)", "s447"], "anchor_clause": "s38(1)", "reference_path": [["s38(1)", "s447"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s38(1) names the offence but states no penalty, only 'liable for action under section 447'. The sentence itself is in s447, whose heading is 'Punishment for fraud' and which shares almost no vocabulary with the question."}
{"id": "CA13-002", "question": "By when must a company file its financial statements with the Registrar before the daily fine starts running?", "answer": "Within the time specified in section 403, which allows filing within the period set by the relevant provision, and late filing within a further 270 days on payment of additional fee. Past that, the company is fined one thousand rupees per day, capped at ten lakh rupees.", "gold_clauses": ["s137(3)", "s403"], "anchor_clause": "s137(3)", "reference_path": [["s137(3)", "s403"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The penalty is in s137(3) but the deadline it hinges on is only in s403. Asking about the deadline retrieves s137(3), which does not contain it."}
{"id": "CA13-003", "question": "Does the Board need a special resolution from shareholders to contribute company funds to the National Defence Fund?", "answer": "No. Section 183(1) lets the Board contribute any amount it thinks fit notwithstanding sections 180, 181 and 182, so the special-resolution requirement that would otherwise apply under section 180 is displaced.", "gold_clauses": ["s183(1)", "s180"], "anchor_clause": "s183(1)", "reference_path": [["s183(1)", "s180"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Good discriminating case: reading s180 alone gives the opposite answer. The 'notwithstanding' override is what makes the reference consequential."}
{"id": "CA13-004", "question": "Can an independent director be granted stock options as part of their remuneration?", "answer": "No. Under section 149(9) an independent director is not entitled to any stock option, and may receive only sitting fees, reimbursement of expenses and profit-related commission, subject to the limits in sections 197 and 198.", "gold_clauses": ["s149(9)", "s197", "s198"], "anchor_clause": "s149(9)", "reference_path": [["s149(9)", "s197"], ["s149(9)", "s198"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Three gold clauses from one anchor. The prohibition is answerable from s149(9) alone, but the permitted remuneration is bounded by s197 and computed under s198."}
{"id": "CA13-005", "question": "A company gives false information in its incorporation application. What are the consequences for the promoters who signed it?", "answer": "The promoters, the persons named as first directors and those making the declaration are liable for action for fraud under section 447, which carries imprisonment of six months to ten years and a fine of not less than the amount involved, up to three times that amount.", "gold_clauses": ["s7(5)", "s7(6)", "s447"], "anchor_clause": "s7(5)", "reference_path": [["s7(5)", "s447"], ["s7(6)", "s447"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Two anchor subsections converge on the same target. s447 is the most-referenced clause in the Act (in-degree 14) and is unreachable from the question wording by semantic similarity."}
